---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Scope and third-party caveats (not amendments to the MIT terms above)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

The MIT grant above covers the new native-project contributions only to the
extent that their contributors hold the necessary rights. It is not a blanket
license for everything found in this repository or a binary archive. Existing
third-party notices and permissions retain their own scope and conditions.

1. Original emulator and source-guided adaptations

The original emulator was designed and programmed by Henrik A. Jacobsen
(Henrik Ascanius Jacobsen). He has permitted use of his own work with credit;
this does not transfer his rights or make him the author of the new emulator.
The pinned V_1.2_DB reference has its own MIT notice, retained in
doc/impl/V_1.2_DB_LICENSE.txt in the source tree and licenses/ in binary archives.
That notice is applicable reference evidence, not a license assignment for all
upstream versions or all historical software. Preserve applicable attribution.

2. Historical monitors, disk images and guest software

MONITOR, STDMON, UNIT0, UNIT0.original, monsrc/, historical listings and guest
programs include material originating with Regnecentralen, RC/ICL and other
parties. Adapted guest utilities may likewise contain historical material.
These materials are excluded from the new-contribution MIT grant. Their
copyrights, notices and any existing permissions remain with the relevant
rights holders. Public availability in an archive does not by itself grant
redistribution permission. Unresolved rights must be reviewed before public
distribution; merely building a binary archive does not resolve them.

3. Third-party libraries and combined binaries

GNU Readline is a separate dependency licensed under GNU GPL version 3 or
later, not under this MIT grant. System libraries are not bundled by the
binary-distribution target. Linking a system-provided library does not by
itself settle the applicable conditions for distributing a combined executable.
The Readline license text is retained in licenses/GPL-3.0.txt. Review the
applicable GPL terms, including source-related obligations, before publishing
binaries. This MIT grant for new contributions does not override them.

Other third-party software, documentation and historical source quotations
retain their own rights and terms; inclusion is not permission to relicense
them. The packaged manual is a compiled document, not a bundle of its fonts
or of the TeX tools used to build it.

The source tree's doc/impl/PROVENANCE_REVIEW.md records the engineering
provenance and open permission questions. These caveats identify scope;
they are not a new permission from a third-party rights holder.
